 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Training convergence is 34% faster than standard 3DGS with the half-cosine kernel [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Memory usage is reduced by 15% compared to standard 3DGS with the half-cosine kernel [S1].
-*   **Performance:** Achieves comparable PSNR, SSIM, and LPIPS results to 3DGS on Mip-NeRF 360 scenes [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** More efficient training and lower memory footprint; wider-spread kernels (half-cosine, Inverse MQ) reduce the need for multiple splats to achieve the same accumulated value [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires precomputed correction factors for non-Gaussian kernels; not all DARBFs perform equally (e.g., radial cosine had lower SSIM than half-cosine in Table 6) [S1].
-*   **When to use:** When computational savings (training time, memory) are critical and comparable visual quality to 3DGS is acceptable.
-*   **When not to use:** When the absolute highest fidelity of the Gaussian kernel's integration properties is required without approximation [S1].
